 @thetechrealist  ​ @The Tech Realist  I literally have a second 27" 2560x1440 monitor sitting beside. I don't use it at 1080p because it's blurrier to downscale.  A 34" 2560x1080 monitor illustrates the point EVEN MORE than if I tested a 21" or 23" monitor, because the differences would be less noticable due to their higher DPI. No your point does not stand. DLSS at its latest iteration (2.3.5) at a proper sharpness level, at Quality mode, at 1080p, is almost always practically on par during normal gameplay, and even on a few cases, superior to native 1080p (i.e. RDR2) with TAA. 

The only place DLSS falls short of TAA at native mostly is the subtle ghosting (some implementations of TAA also exhibit this). And in rare cases, in-game settings tied to render resolution have to be bumped up another notch up to match native quality.

I've tested several games, with several diferent DLSS versions in most of each. The only point you do have is what I mentioned in the prior "paragraph". Otherwise, DLSS being "shit" at 1080p at quality mode, at the current 2.3.5 version, is simply false.

