 @mirage8753  The 6600 and the 3060 are basically neck-and-neck at rasterization, but the 3060 offers DLSS and 50% more VRAM as well as superior raytracing (meh) and video encoding. At the same MSRP ($330), had both been available at that price, nobody (except us Linuxers) would have spared the 6600 a thought.

The only reason it currently deserves consideration (which it does) is because the price-gouging is less egregious than on the 3060, though it could be said to reflect the real value of the cards. From that perspective, both could be said to currently be priced about double a more "realistic" MSRP, at $560 and $700 respectively.

Disclaimer: "Proud" RX 6600 owner, bought on release day for $370.

